WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Go input validation requires explicit runtime enforcement at trust boundaries.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:13.400
Static typing, structs, JSON decoding, and simple control flow are valuable, but they do not prove that external data is safe.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:26.060
Every important entry point should define what it accepts, decode carefully, limit resource use, handle missing and zero values intentionally, and reject invalid input before it reaches sensitive code.

4
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.780
Strong validation in Go starts with clear contracts.

5
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:43.240
Required and optional fields, string length, numeric ranges, allowed values, nested structs, slices, maps, and cross-field rules should be defined before processing.

6
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.620
JSON decoding should not be confused with validation.

7
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:58.000
Unknown fields, body size, defaults, missing values, custom unmarshalling, and decoded zero values all need deliberate handling based on the API contract.

8
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.200
Parsing is only the beginning.

9
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:09.020
Numbers, dates, times, identifiers, file names, paths, and structured files need checks after conversion succeeds.

10
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.280
A parsed number may be out of range.

11
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.020
A parsed time may violate an expiration or scheduling rule.

12
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.240
A UUID-shaped value may not belong to the caller.

13
00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.100
A cleaned path may still need containment checks.

14
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.880
The meaning of the value matters as much as its syntax.

15
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.040
The final takeaway is predictable behavior.

16
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:39.260
Good Go validation rejects malformed, oversized, incomplete, unexpected, and business-invalid input early and safely.

17
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.240
Good tests cover negative cases and boundaries, not just happy paths.

18
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.580
Good reviews trace raw input from the boundary into trusted application logic.

19
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:59.240
When validation is explicit, Go's simplicity becomes a strength: the rules are visible, the failures are controlled, and sensitive operations receive data they can safely reason about.
